+### Connection establishment and the first message
+
+Before waiting for client bytes or a handshake to choose/connect an upstream, inspect the
+actual client/server handshake and selected proxy filters. Record who speaks first and when
+the routing metadata becomes available. For example, MySQL's classic handshake sends a server
+greeting before the client response, including before its optional TLS request; it does not
+start with a TLS ClientHello merely because TLS is required later.
+
+If the client waits for that greeting while the proxy waits for client data before opening
+the upstream connection, neither can progress. Increasing the timeout only prolongs the wait.
+Use a listener or other trusted route that can connect before the greeting, or a supported
+protocol-aware intermediary with an explicit handshake contract. Do not fabricate a greeting
+or silently change TLS/authentication to make a generic byte-forwarder work. Deferred connection
+can still suit a client-first protocol when the needed metadata is available and inspection
+time/buffering are bounded; check the deployed version's mechanism rather than copying a flag.
+
+Test actual handshake completion, not just TCP connect or a healthy listener. Include a client
+that waits for a server greeting, a client-first control, and both the deferred and immediate
+connection paths; correlate bytes and upstream connection creation to distinguish circular
+waiting from upstream refusal or slowness.
+
